  "explanation": "The proposal aims to optimize multi-agent LLM dialogues for scientific ideation by combining token-space feedback loops with retrieved literature snippets to handle novelty and feasibility constraints. However, the core concepts—such as multi-agent iterative refinement loops (generator-critic), retrieval-augmented novelty checking, and prompt-guided steering—are heavily explored in existing literature (e.g., SciMON, multi-agent ideation frameworks, and retrieval-based novelty scoring). The specific focus on avoiding medium-novelty biases and relying purely on prompt interventions shares close conceptual territory with recent prompt-guided and adversarial multi-agent ideation strategies, rendering the incremental novelty relatively modest.",
  "problems": [
    "The core mechanism of generator-critic multi-agent loops with retrieval integration heavily overlaps with established frameworks like SciMON and multi-agent virtual scientist architectures.",
    "The proposal claims to solve medium-novelty bias through prompt-based contrastive feedback, but lacks a clear theoretical distinction from existing iterative reflection and critique methods used in multi-agent generation.",
    "Relying solely on zero-shot scoring heuristics and prompts for fine-grained novelty control with an 8B quantized model on a single 12GB GPU may suffer from severe model capability bottlenecks when executing complex scientific reasoning tasks."
  ],
  "improvements": [
    "Differentiate the proposed token-space intervention loop explicitly from recent work on hidden-state probing (such as ThinkProbe-Respond) or fine-grained reward models to clarify its unique technical contribution.",
    "Incorporate specific algorithmic details on how the zero-shot scoring heuristics avoid degenerate or hallucinated novelty scores when utilizing a smaller 8B base model.",
    "Provide a concrete ablation comparing standard multi-agent critique loops against the proposed contrastive prompt-guided refinement loop to isolate the true gain of the intervention."
  ],
